{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And she is a slave-girl designated (נֶחֱרֶפֶת) to a man.</b> Designated (מְיֹעֶדֶת) to a man — and I do not know of any parallel to it in Scripture; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 19:20). But it appears to me that it is from the language of \"as I was in the days of my חָרְפִּי\" (Job 29:4) — \"in the days of my youth.\" And likewise \"my heart will not יֶחֱרַף from my days\" (Job 27:6), meaning: my heart will not be that of a youth. And the days of youth are called days of חֹרֶף, because חֹרֶף (winter/early rain) is at the beginning of the years, while the days of old age correspond to קַיִץ (summer), which are the days of gathering (אָסִיף). Scripture says that she is a שִׁפְחָה נַעֲרָה — a young female servant — belonging to a designated man. For a concubine who serves the man and lies with him is called his נַעֲרָה, for every servant of a person is also called his נַעַר. And it is customary in the language of the Sages to say of a woman who lies with a man that she is מְשַׁמֶּשֶׁת עִמּוֹ. And she is נֶחֱרֶפֶת, meaning she has become a נַעֲרָה to a man and is designated (וִידוּעָה) to him — in the foreign tongue: espoused (מסיפד\"ה). And likewise in the language of the Sages (Sanhedrin 48a), they call her רָבִיתָא דִּפְלָנְיָא. The meaning is that she is not his wife in every respect, but he gave her קִדּוּשִׁין, and she is to him as a נַעֲרָה מְשַׁמֶּשֶׁת." "<b>She shall be בִּקֹּרֶת.</b> It is incumbent upon the court to investigate (לְבַקֵּר) the matter so as not to impose the death penalty — \"for she was not חֻפָּשָׁה\" and her קִדּוּשִׁין are not complete; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 19:20). And so said all the commentators. If so, Scripture warns about this investigation because she resembles an married woman (אֵשֶׁת אִישׁ), and they might err concerning her. But their explanation is not smooth on the tongue, for it is self-evident that the matter will be investigated and they will not put a person to death without cause — and so they would investigate in all cases subject to the death penalty. But I hold that it is a unique word in Scripture, yet it is current in Aramaic, and in the words of our Sages it is from the root הֶפְקֵר, for the root of the word is הֶבְקֵר. As they said (Pe'ah 6:1): Bet Shammai say: הֶבְקֵר for the poor is הֶבְקֵר, and Bet Hillel say: until it is declared ownerless (עַד שֶׁיַּבְקִיר) even for the rich, like the Sabbatical year. And we also learned (Shevi'it 9:4): They may eat what is מֻבְקָר but not what is שָׁמוּר. And Targum Jonathan translated \"empty and reckless men\" (Judges 9:4) as \"סְרִיקִין וּבָקְרִין,\" that is, men of הֶבְקֵר, and \"by their falsehood and recklessness\" (Jeremiah 23:32) as \"וּבַקָּרוּתְהוֹן.\" And they said that \"you shall let it rest and lay it aside (וּנְטַשְׁתָּהּ)\" (Exodus 23:11) is translated in the Jerusalem Targum as \"וּתְבַקְּרִנַּהּ.\" And so everywhere in the Mishnah the root is with a bet, but in the Gemara they are accustomed to say from it הֶפְקֵר, for these letters are always interchanged among them, as they say (Pesachim 31b) from \"the עֲבָשׁוּ granaries\" (Joel 1:17) — \"עִפּוּשׁ.\" And likewise in Scripture in many places, as I have noted in connection with the verse \"You blew with Your wind (נָשַׁפְתָּ בְּרוּחֲךָ)\" (Exodus 15:10). And the meaning of this verse as stated about this slave-girl is: even though she is נֶחֱרֶפֶת to a man, she shall not be his wife, for she shall be to him בִּקֹּרֶת — that is, she is reckoned מֻפְקֶרֶת (ownerless/abandoned) with respect to him, and they shall not be put to death according to the law of the adulterer and adulteress with an married woman, for she was not חֻפָּשָׁה to be his wife in full; but he shall bring a guilt-offering for her, because she is נֶחֱרֶפֶת to him. And Onkelos (Onkelos on Leviticus 19:20), who said \"בִּקֻּרְתָּא תְּהֵא,\" his language is like the language of Targum Jonathan ben Uzziel (Judges 9:4) in the word \"בָּקְרִין.\""], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The Romance word transcribed in Hebrew characters as מסיפד\"ה appears to derive from Old French 'espouser/e spouse/de,' meaning betrothed or espoused. The exact form and language are uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:56:44.862173887Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}